WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.653
Welcome to AI-Native Mobility Marketplace. For the next half hour, we will follow one rider, Aisha,

2
00:00:05.653 --> 00:00:09.539
as she asks for a ride home during a busy evening.

3
00:00:09.539 --> 00:00:13.072
Our goal is not to copy any company's internal system.

4
00:00:13.072 --> 00:00:17.665
We are designing an original marketplace that performs the same kind of job:

5
00:00:17.665 --> 00:00:21.198
connect a rider and a driver safely, quickly, and fairly.

6
00:00:21.198 --> 00:00:24.731
Along the way, we will build a reusable interview method.

7
00:00:24.731 --> 00:00:31.090
System design is the practice of choosing components and rules that let a product work reliably at scale.

8
00:00:31.090 --> 00:00:32.150
In plain English,

9
00:00:32.150 --> 00:00:37.803
it is the blueprint for how many moving parts cooperate when real people depend on them.

10
00:00:37.803 --> 00:00:43.809
A good blueprint starts with the user story, then asks what can fail, what must be fast,

11
00:00:43.809 --> 00:00:49.815
and which facts must never be wrong. Notice the order: we do not begin with fashionable tools.

12
00:00:49.815 --> 00:00:51.935
We begin with promises to people,

13
00:00:51.935 --> 00:00:58.294
then prove that our choices can keep those promises under ordinary load and stressful failure. By the end,

14
00:00:58.294 --> 00:01:04.654
you will be able to explain the ride flow to a beginner and defend the choices in an

15
00:01:04.654 --> 00:01:11.700
interview. Let’s follow one ride request from the first tap to the final receipt.

16
00:01:11.700 --> 00:01:14.953
Aisha opens the rider app outside a concert.

17
00:01:14.953 --> 00:01:20.239
She enters a pickup point, chooses a destination, and asks for a price.

18
00:01:20.239 --> 00:01:26.745
Marco is driving nearby and may receive an offer. The third participant is our marketplace platform,

19
00:01:26.745 --> 00:01:32.031
which coordinates the request without pretending it can control traffic or human decisions.

20
00:01:32.031 --> 00:01:37.724
An actor is a person or external system that participates in a product flow.

21
00:01:37.724 --> 00:01:43.010
Here, Aisha, Marco, the mapping provider, and the payment provider are all actors.

22
00:01:43.010 --> 00:01:49.923
Naming actors matters because each has different needs. Aisha wants a clear price and a safe pickup.

23
00:01:49.923 --> 00:01:53.989
Marco needs a useful offer before another driver takes it.

24
00:01:53.989 --> 00:01:59.275
The mapping provider supplies routes, while the payment provider handles regulated money movement.

25
00:01:59.275 --> 00:02:05.374
If we blur those responsibilities, our design becomes vague and failures become hard to explain.

26
00:02:05.374 --> 00:02:07.407
Keep Aisha’s story in mind:

27
00:02:07.407 --> 00:02:15.200
every box we add should solve a problem she or Marco can actually feel.

28
00:02:15.200 --> 00:02:19.897
At the moment Aisha requests, three drivers appear close on the map.

29
00:02:19.897 --> 00:02:24.595
One is turning away from the pickup. One is finishing a ride.

30
00:02:24.595 --> 00:02:31.641
Marco is slightly farther away but is free, facing the right direction, and has a fresh location update.

31
00:02:31.641 --> 00:02:33.598
Distance alone is not enough.

32
00:02:33.598 --> 00:02:39.861
A strong match balances pickup time, whether a driver can truly respond, rider safety, cancellation risk,

33
00:02:39.861 --> 00:02:42.210
and a fair distribution of opportunities.

34
00:02:42.210 --> 00:02:49.256
Prediction question: is the nearest map dot always the best driver for Aisha? Take a moment to decide.

35
00:02:49.256 --> 00:02:55.128
The answer is no. A stale dot may represent a driver who is already occupied,

36
00:02:55.128 --> 00:02:59.434
and a very close driver may be headed the wrong way.

37
00:02:59.434 --> 00:03:05.697
Without a matching process that considers live constraints, riders see failed offers, drivers get confusing work,

38
00:03:05.697 --> 00:03:07.654
and the marketplace loses trust.

39
00:03:07.654 --> 00:03:19.800
So we will first make the product promises clear, then build only the machinery needed to keep them.

40
00:03:19.800 --> 00:03:24.640
Before naming storage or models, write down what the product must do.

41
00:03:24.640 --> 00:03:31.497
A functional requirement is a specific capability the product must provide to a user or another system.

42
00:03:31.497 --> 00:03:37.548
For Aisha, the platform must produce a quote, accept her ride request, find a driver,

43
00:03:37.548 --> 00:03:43.195
show the trip progressing, settle payment, and let either person report a safety concern.

44
00:03:43.195 --> 00:03:46.018
Those are observable outcomes, not implementation choices.

45
00:03:46.018 --> 00:03:52.472
The phrase ‘match a driver’ is a useful requirement; the phrase ‘use a queue’ is not,

46
00:03:52.472 --> 00:03:58.119
because it jumps ahead to a solution. This distinction keeps an interview answer grounded.

47
00:03:58.119 --> 00:04:04.572
If we skip functional requirements, we may optimize an impressive architecture that forgets a vital action,

48
00:04:04.572 --> 00:04:09.009
such as expiring an unanswered offer or preventing a second charge.

49
00:04:09.009 --> 00:04:13.043
In this lesson, we exclude pooled rides and autonomous vehicles.

50
00:04:13.043 --> 00:04:24.000
Narrow scope is a strength: it lets us make the normal ride dependable before adding harder variations.

51
00:04:24.000 --> 00:04:27.364
Now ask how well each capability must work.

52
00:04:27.364 --> 00:04:32.830
A non-functional requirement describes a quality target such as speed, reliability, or security.

53
00:04:32.830 --> 00:04:37.455
It turns ‘fast’ into a number and ‘reliable’ into a promise.

54
00:04:37.455 --> 00:04:43.762
For example, we want Aisha’s quote to return with p99 latency under five hundred milliseconds.

55
00:04:43.762 --> 00:04:49.228
P99 latency is the response time below which 99 percent of requests complete:

56
00:04:49.228 --> 00:04:55.115
if one hundred quote requests arrive, at least ninety-nine should finish within that limit.

57
00:04:55.115 --> 00:05:01.001
The consequence is that a slow dependency cannot sit on the critical path forever.

58
00:05:01.001 --> 00:05:06.467
Availability is the proportion of time a system can successfully serve intended requests.

59
00:05:06.467 --> 00:05:09.411
If availability is poor at commute time,

60
00:05:09.411 --> 00:05:14.456
a beautiful prediction model is irrelevant because Aisha cannot request a ride.

61
00:05:14.456 --> 00:05:21.604
We also set match completion under five seconds, location freshness under ten seconds, and no double charge.

62
00:05:21.604 --> 00:05:27.068
These targets will guide every trade-off that follows.

63
00:05:27.068 --> 00:05:31.418
Not every fact in a marketplace needs the same agreement rule.

64
00:05:31.418 --> 00:05:38.140
Strong consistency means a completed write is immediately reflected by later reads that require the same truth.

65
00:05:38.140 --> 00:05:41.699
Think of one shared ledger: after Aisha’s payment succeeds,

66
00:05:41.699 --> 00:05:46.048
a later payment check must not claim it is still unpaid.

67
00:05:46.048 --> 00:05:50.793
The consequence is that trip assignment and money movement need careful coordination,

68
00:05:50.793 --> 00:05:53.561
even if that costs a little latency.

69
00:05:53.561 --> 00:05:58.702
Eventual consistency means copies may briefly differ but converge when updates finish propagating.

70
00:05:58.702 --> 00:06:05.820
A driver’s moving map dot can tolerate a small delay because the next update will arrive seconds later.

71
00:06:05.820 --> 00:06:12.542
Prediction question: should every ride datum wait for one shared truth? Take a moment to decide. No.

72
00:06:12.542 --> 00:06:18.473
If every location update waited for global agreement, the map would lag and cost more.

73
00:06:18.473 --> 00:06:22.823
If payment used only eventual agreement, we could charge Aisha twice.

74
00:06:22.823 --> 00:06:32.668
Use the strongest promise only where an incorrect answer would be unacceptable.

75
00:06:32.668 --> 00:06:35.771
A diagram without numbers is only a guess.

76
00:06:35.771 --> 00:06:41.201
A capacity estimate is a transparent approximation of expected requests, data, and storage needs.

77
00:06:41.201 --> 00:06:44.304
It is not a claim of perfect forecasting;

78
00:06:44.304 --> 00:06:50.510
it is a way to show your assumptions and test whether a design can survive them.

79
00:06:50.510 --> 00:06:55.165
We will assume two million trips per day, one million active drivers,

80
00:06:55.165 --> 00:06:58.656
and a commute peak ten times busier than average.

81
00:06:58.656 --> 00:07:05.250
We will calculate ride requests, location updates, and retained history separately because they have very different shapes.

82
00:07:05.250 --> 00:07:09.129
Aisha creates one important command when she requests a trip.

83
00:07:09.129 --> 00:07:14.171
Marco creates a steady stream of lightweight location updates while he is available.

84
00:07:14.171 --> 00:07:19.601
If we size the system only for trip requests, the location stream overwhelms it.

85
00:07:19.601 --> 00:07:23.092
If we size every record like a location sample,

86
00:07:23.092 --> 00:07:27.747
we waste effort on the few trip transitions that demand durable correctness.

87
00:07:27.747 --> 00:07:33.953
Start with questions: how many users, how often do they act, how big is each message,

88
00:07:33.953 --> 00:07:40.768
and what happens at the busiest hour?

89
00:07:40.768 --> 00:07:43.917
Let’s do the first piece of math slowly.

90
00:07:43.917 --> 00:07:51.003
Two million trips per day divided by eighty-six thousand four hundred seconds in a day is about twenty-three

91
00:07:51.003 --> 00:07:53.365
trip creations per second on average.

92
00:07:53.365 --> 00:08:00.058
Throughput is the amount of work a system completes in a fixed time, often requests per second.

93
00:08:00.058 --> 00:08:04.782
Average throughput is useful, but it is not what stresses the platform.

94
00:08:04.782 --> 00:08:10.293
Peak load is the highest expected demand period used to size a system safely.

95
00:08:10.293 --> 00:08:16.592
With a ten-times commute peak, we plan for about two hundred thirty trip creations per second.

96
00:08:16.592 --> 00:08:23.678
That is modest enough for a well-designed regional transactional path, but every trip creates related work: quotes, offers,

97
00:08:23.678 --> 00:08:26.040
state changes, notifications, and later payments.

98
00:08:26.040 --> 00:08:31.158
The interview move is to say the arithmetic aloud and label the assumption.

99
00:08:31.158 --> 00:08:35.488
If peak is actually twenty times average in a dense city,

100
00:08:35.488 --> 00:08:39.031
we can scale regional capacity or shed nonessential work.

101
00:08:39.031 --> 00:08:45.068
Hidden math produces hidden outages; transparent math creates a conversation.

102
00:08:45.068 --> 00:08:47.417
The second calculation changes the scale.

103
00:08:47.417 --> 00:08:52.506
One million active drivers send one roughly one-hundred-byte location update every four seconds.

104
00:08:52.506 --> 00:08:58.769
That is two hundred fifty thousand updates per second at peak: one million divided by four.

105
00:08:58.769 --> 00:09:03.857
An event is an immutable record that something happened at a particular time.

106
00:09:03.857 --> 00:09:07.381
Marco’s position at a given time is an event;

107
00:09:07.381 --> 00:09:11.686
it is not the same as rewriting the entire trip record.

108
00:09:11.686 --> 00:09:14.818
At two hundred fifty thousand events per second,

109
00:09:14.818 --> 00:09:20.298
raw payload volume is about twenty-five megabytes per second before protocol overhead or copies.

110
00:09:20.298 --> 00:09:25.387
This is why a marketplace cannot treat moving positions like ordinary account edits.

111
00:09:25.387 --> 00:09:31.650
We need a path that accepts a burst, keeps the most recent location easy to find,

112
00:09:31.650 --> 00:09:34.782
and stores history more cheaply. Without that separation,

113
00:09:34.782 --> 00:09:41.828
the large location stream competes with Aisha’s small but critical ride request and turns the core record store

114
00:09:41.828 --> 00:09:46.668
into a bottleneck precisely when demand rises.

115
00:09:46.668 --> 00:09:50.268
Now let storage follow the meaning of the data.

116
00:09:50.268 --> 00:09:55.468
Live positions are hot: dispatch needs Marco’s current cell and a recent timestamp,

117
00:09:55.468 --> 00:10:01.868
not every point from last week. We keep that compact, indexed state with a short expiration.

118
00:10:01.868 --> 00:10:07.468
Raw historical telemetry can move to lower-cost object storage for approved analysis and retention.

119
00:10:07.468 --> 00:10:12.268
Seven days of raw locations is roughly fifteen terabytes under our assumptions;

120
00:10:12.268 --> 00:10:17.068
with three copies plus index and metadata overhead, budget around fifty terabytes.

121
00:10:17.068 --> 00:10:23.868
Replication keeps multiple copies of data so a failure of one copy does not make it unavailable.

122
00:10:23.868 --> 00:10:30.268
The practical consequence is different for different data: trip and payment state need durable replicated truth,

123
00:10:30.268 --> 00:10:33.868
while an old map dot can expire. Recap question:

124
00:10:33.868 --> 00:10:40.668
where would you store Marco’s last ten seconds of position versus a week of approved route history?

125
00:10:40.668 --> 00:10:46.668
Take a moment. Keep the fresh position close to dispatch; keep history durable and cheaper.

126
00:10:46.668 --> 00:10:58.968
Without these layers, either the hot path becomes slow or the storage bill grows without improving Aisha’s ride.

127
00:10:58.968 --> 00:11:03.417
We have enough requirements and scale to draw the outside boundary.

128
00:11:03.417 --> 00:11:07.461
The rider app and driver app are on one side.

129
00:11:07.461 --> 00:11:12.314
On the other side are a routing provider, a licensed payment provider,

130
00:11:12.314 --> 00:11:15.954
and emergency or safety services governed by explicit policy.

131
00:11:15.954 --> 00:11:20.807
Our platform sits in the middle and decides which interactions it owns.

132
00:11:20.807 --> 00:11:26.469
It owns trip state, matching, location handling, and the audit trail around safety workflow.

133
00:11:26.469 --> 00:11:33.344
It does not pretend to own road maps or card networks. This context diagram is intentionally simple.

134
00:11:33.344 --> 00:11:40.624
It helps an interviewer see where data enters, where trust boundaries exist, and where a dependency might fail.

135
00:11:40.624 --> 00:11:46.286
For Aisha, routing gives an estimated path. For Marco, the app receives an offer.

136
00:11:46.286 --> 00:11:50.330
For both, payment and emergency actions have stricter external obligations.

137
00:11:50.330 --> 00:11:54.374
If we open the architecture before agreeing on these boundaries,

138
00:11:54.374 --> 00:11:58.419
we risk hiding a vendor dependency inside an unexplained box.

139
00:11:58.419 --> 00:12:06.563
Next, we build the inside one layer at a time.

140
00:12:06.563 --> 00:12:09.598
Aisha’s request enters through an API edge.

141
00:12:09.598 --> 00:12:16.101
An API edge is the controlled entry layer that authenticates, routes, and protects client requests.

142
00:12:16.101 --> 00:12:23.905
In everyday terms, it is the guarded front door: it checks who is calling, rejects obviously bad traffic,

143
00:12:23.905 --> 00:12:27.374
and sends valid calls to the right place.

144
00:12:27.374 --> 00:12:33.877
Behind it, a service is a focused deployable component responsible for one bounded business capability.

145
00:12:33.877 --> 00:12:41.681
We separate trip, location, dispatch, payment, and safety responsibilities so one change does not accidentally rewrite every rule.

146
00:12:41.681 --> 00:12:48.618
A regional shard is an isolated deployment and data partition that serves a geographic area independently.

147
00:12:48.618 --> 00:12:54.687
Imagine a city-sized neighborhood of the platform with its own capacity and recovery plan.

148
00:12:54.687 --> 00:13:02.058
Its consequence is containment: a failure in one region should not make another region stop matching rides.

149
00:13:02.058 --> 00:13:06.393
Without an edge, unsafe or malformed traffic reaches every component.

150
00:13:06.393 --> 00:13:14.563
Without focused services and regional shards, a small fault becomes a wide, confusing outage.

151
00:13:14.563 --> 00:13:18.671
The next layer holds durable truth and shares changes safely.

152
00:13:18.671 --> 00:13:24.012
A database is a durable system for storing and querying authoritative application state.

153
00:13:24.012 --> 00:13:29.764
The trip database holds Aisha’s ride status, its version, and the confirmed driver assignment.

154
00:13:29.764 --> 00:13:34.694
It is where we insist that one transition follows the last one.

155
00:13:34.694 --> 00:13:41.267
An event log is a durable ordered record of changes that other components can consume independently.

156
00:13:41.267 --> 00:13:47.840
When the trip changes from requested to matched, the log lets notifications, payment preparation, safety workflows,

157
00:13:47.840 --> 00:13:53.592
and analytics learn about that fact without every component calling the trip service directly.

158
00:13:53.592 --> 00:13:59.343
The consequence is loose coupling: a delayed notification does not block a driver assignment.

159
00:13:59.343 --> 00:14:05.095
We partition both trip data and the log by region, then by trip identifier,

160
00:14:05.095 --> 00:14:12.079
so related work stays close together. Without a durable database, we cannot confidently answer who is assigned.

161
00:14:12.079 --> 00:14:21.163
Without an event log, every new downstream feature makes the synchronous request path slower and more fragile.

162
00:14:21.163 --> 00:14:26.660
Here is a subtle but important failure. Suppose the trip database records that Marco accepted,

163
00:14:26.660 --> 00:14:30.691
but the process crashes just before it tells the event log.

164
00:14:30.691 --> 00:14:36.554
Aisha might wait on a stale screen while payment and safety systems never learn the assignment.

165
00:14:36.554 --> 00:14:43.150
An outbox is a durable record written with business state so its event can be published reliably after

166
00:14:43.150 --> 00:14:49.747
the transaction. Think of it as a message placed in the same sealed envelope as the trip update.

167
00:14:49.747 --> 00:14:54.877
A separate publisher reads the envelope and delivers the event, retrying safely if necessary.

168
00:14:54.877 --> 00:15:00.740
The consequence is that a completed state change does not disappear between two unreliable network calls.

169
00:15:00.740 --> 00:15:06.970
This is more dependable than trying to update the database and send a message as unrelated steps.

170
00:15:06.970 --> 00:15:12.833
Without an outbox, we get the worst kind of bug: the core truth says one thing,

171
00:15:12.833 --> 00:15:21.163
while everything around it silently believes another. It is a small pattern with a large reliability payoff.

172
00:15:21.163 --> 00:15:26.644
Look at our architecture as a set of owners, not a pile of boxes.

173
00:15:26.644 --> 00:15:32.516
The trip component owns the authoritative ride state. The location path owns fresh moving positions.

174
00:15:32.516 --> 00:15:37.605
Dispatch owns the process of proposing an assignment, and payment owns settlement records.

175
00:15:37.605 --> 00:15:43.869
Other components can react to published changes, but they do not casually rewrite another owner’s truth.

176
00:15:43.869 --> 00:15:49.742
Prediction question: should every component edit the same tables to save time? Take a moment.

177
00:15:49.742 --> 00:15:56.397
The answer is no. Shared writes create hidden coupling: a payment change can accidentally alter a trip,

178
00:15:56.397 --> 00:15:59.920
and an emergency repair becomes impossible to reason about.

179
00:15:59.920 --> 00:16:06.184
Clear ownership lets us test a state transition, audit it, and recover it in one place.

180
00:16:06.184 --> 00:16:12.448
It also makes each team or module smaller to understand. For Aisha, the effect is simple:

181
00:16:12.448 --> 00:16:19.495
one part of the platform can be late without allowing two parts to decide she has two different

182
00:16:19.495 --> 00:16:28.901
drivers. Now we can zoom into the hardest owner-to-owner handoff: dispatch.

183
00:16:28.901 --> 00:16:34.099
Aisha taps request, and dispatch needs possible drivers before it can choose one.

184
00:16:34.099 --> 00:16:40.897
Searching every driver in the city would be wasteful. Instead, we divide the map into small hexagons.

185
00:16:40.897 --> 00:16:47.695
An H3 cell is an addressable hexagonal area used to group nearby geographic positions for efficient search.

186
00:16:47.695 --> 00:16:52.094
Think of it as a labeled tile on a honeycomb map.

187
00:16:52.094 --> 00:16:56.492
Marco’s latest position updates the tile where he is currently available.

188
00:16:56.492 --> 00:17:00.891
Aisha’s pickup identifies its own tile, and dispatch searches that tile,

189
00:17:00.891 --> 00:17:04.090
then expands outward in rings only if needed.

190
00:17:04.090 --> 00:17:10.887
Hexagons have convenient neighboring shapes, but the key idea is spatial bucketing, not the brand of index.

191
00:17:10.887 --> 00:17:17.285
The consequence is bounded work: a request looks at nearby candidates rather than a city-wide list.

192
00:17:17.285 --> 00:17:18.885
Without a geographic index,

193
00:17:18.885 --> 00:17:25.683
rush-hour matching becomes a repeated full scan and latency rises just when Aisha most needs an answer.

194
00:17:25.683 --> 00:17:34.981
Next we narrow the nearby list to drivers who can actually take her.

195
00:17:34.981 --> 00:17:37.845
The H3 search returns possibilities, not promises.

196
00:17:37.845 --> 00:17:43.573
A candidate filter quickly removes options that cannot satisfy a request before expensive scoring.

197
00:17:43.573 --> 00:17:49.301
It might remove drivers whose last location is too old, who are already leased,

198
00:17:49.301 --> 00:17:55.439
whose vehicle does not fit the requested service, or who are outside a safety boundary.

199
00:17:55.439 --> 00:18:01.985
Eligibility is the set of non-negotiable rules an option must satisfy before it can be offered.

200
00:18:01.985 --> 00:18:06.076
In plain English, eligibility is the bouncer at the door;

201
00:18:06.076 --> 00:18:10.986
no amount of clever prediction lets an unavailable driver accept a ride.

202
00:18:10.986 --> 00:18:17.942
Marco passes because he is active, his location is fresh, and he is not finishing another trip.

203
00:18:17.942 --> 00:18:20.397
The consequence is efficiency and safety:

204
00:18:20.397 --> 00:18:27.761
our ranking model sees a manageable set of viable choices instead of trying to repair impossible ones later.

205
00:18:27.761 --> 00:18:34.717
Without this filter, the platform may offer Aisha a driver who cannot respond, causing timeouts and cancellations.

206
00:18:34.717 --> 00:18:39.681
Hard rules come first; preference comes after.

207
00:18:39.681 --> 00:18:43.514
Among viable drivers, we choose the most useful offer.

208
00:18:43.514 --> 00:18:49.477
An online feature is a fresh input, such as current traffic or driver state,

209
00:18:49.477 --> 00:18:51.606
used during a live decision.

210
00:18:51.606 --> 00:18:58.847
For Marco and the other candidates, features might include route time, recent acceptance behavior, rain, road closures,

211
00:18:58.847 --> 00:19:01.828
and how long each driver has waited.

212
00:19:01.828 --> 00:19:09.494
A learned ranker is a model that orders options by predicted usefulness using historical patterns and current inputs.

213
00:19:09.494 --> 00:19:15.457
It can estimate pickup time, acceptance chance, and cancellation risk better than distance alone.

214
00:19:15.457 --> 00:19:19.290
But no model gets to be the only path.

215
00:19:19.290 --> 00:19:26.956
A deterministic scorer ranks options with fixed, explainable rules that produce the same result for the same inputs.

216
00:19:26.956 --> 00:19:30.789
Ours combines map ETA, distance, and a fairness adjustment.

217
00:19:30.789 --> 00:19:37.604
The consequence is graceful operation: if inference is late, low-confidence, too costly, or disallowed by policy,

218
00:19:37.604 --> 00:19:45.270
dispatch still makes a transparent choice. Without a fallback, a model incident would turn into a marketplace outage.

219
00:19:45.270 --> 00:19:52.481
AI improves the choice; it never becomes permission to stop serving Aisha.

220
00:19:52.481 --> 00:19:57.210
Ranking one driver is not enough when many riders ask at once.

221
00:19:57.210 --> 00:20:04.304
An optimizer selects the best allowed assignment across options while respecting constraints such as fairness and detour limits.

222
00:20:04.304 --> 00:20:08.639
Rather than greedily give every nearby request the same attractive driver,

223
00:20:08.639 --> 00:20:15.733
it considers a small batch of offers and chooses a combination that gives the marketplace a better overall

224
00:20:15.733 --> 00:20:21.644
result. We keep the optimization bounded so it finishes inside the match target; under overload,

225
00:20:21.644 --> 00:20:28.738
we can fall back to a simpler greedy rule. After selecting Marco for Aisha, dispatch creates a lease.

226
00:20:28.738 --> 00:20:34.649
A lease is a time-limited reservation that grants one worker temporary ownership of a resource.

227
00:20:34.649 --> 00:20:40.561
It says, for example, that Marco is reserved for Aisha’s offer for a short window.

228
00:20:40.561 --> 00:20:46.866
The consequence is no double booking: another rider cannot win the same driver while Marco decides.

229
00:20:46.866 --> 00:20:55.666
Without a lease, two dispatch workers may race, both send offers, and create an impossible real-world conflict.

230
00:20:55.666 --> 00:20:58.275
Let’s pause at the decision point.

231
00:20:58.275 --> 00:21:04.797
The dispatch pipeline is map search, hard eligibility rules, scoring, bounded assignment, reservation, and offer.

232
00:21:04.797 --> 00:21:10.450
It is deliberately a sequence from cheap, certain checks to more sophisticated choices.

233
00:21:10.450 --> 00:21:16.972
Prediction question: where should a slow model stop Aisha’s ride? Take a moment to answer.

234
00:21:16.972 --> 00:21:18.711
It should stop nowhere.

235
00:21:18.711 --> 00:21:26.103
If the learned ranker exceeds its latency budget, returns low confidence, or cannot use a sensitive input,

236
00:21:26.103 --> 00:21:28.277
the deterministic fallback takes over.

237
00:21:28.277 --> 00:21:36.103
A deterministic fallback is a fixed safe path used when a model is slow, unavailable, low-confidence, or disallowed.

238
00:21:36.103 --> 00:21:42.625
Here it uses route time, distance, availability, and fairness rules to select a viable driver.

239
00:21:42.625 --> 00:21:48.713
The model result may improve efficiency, but the fixed path protects the product promise.

240
00:21:48.713 --> 00:21:56.105
This boundary is the core of an AI-native design: models are valuable advisors inside a dependable system,

241
00:21:56.105 --> 00:21:59.148
not fragile gatekeepers in front of it.

242
00:21:59.148 --> 00:22:09.466
Now we will trace exactly how Aisha’s request moves through that system.

243
00:22:09.466 --> 00:22:16.614
Aisha first asks for a quote, which returns a price range, an ETA, and an expiration time.

244
00:22:16.614 --> 00:22:22.080
When she confirms, the app sends a trip command with an idempotency key.

245
00:22:22.080 --> 00:22:27.967
Idempotency means repeating the same command has the same effect as performing it once.

246
00:22:27.967 --> 00:22:33.013
In plain language, if her train-station network drops just after the tap,

247
00:22:33.013 --> 00:22:36.797
the app can retry without accidentally creating two rides.

248
00:22:36.797 --> 00:22:43.525
The trip service stores the request, records an outbox item, and asks dispatch for a match.

249
00:22:43.525 --> 00:22:48.991
Dispatch searches H3 cells, filters candidates, scores them, reserves Marco with a lease,

250
00:22:48.991 --> 00:22:56.560
and sends his app an offer. If the model path is unhealthy, it uses the deterministic route instead.

251
00:22:56.560 --> 00:22:57.401
Marco accepts;

252
00:22:57.401 --> 00:23:04.970
the trip service confirms the assignment using a version check so a late duplicate acceptance cannot overwrite the

253
00:23:04.970 --> 00:23:12.538
truth. Aisha sees Marco’s car and ETA. Each transition creates an event for the rest of the platform.

254
00:23:12.538 --> 00:23:23.366
Without idempotency and version checks, ordinary mobile retries become double trips, duplicated offers, and support tickets.

255
00:23:23.366 --> 00:23:30.719
Real systems fail in uneven ways. During a concert exit, location updates may surge before trip requests do.

256
00:23:30.719 --> 00:23:37.663
Backpressure is a controlled slowdown that keeps a fast producer from overwhelming a slower consumer. For example,

257
00:23:37.663 --> 00:23:45.016
the platform can lower noncritical location sampling or coalesce repeated points before it rejects an important ride command.

258
00:23:45.016 --> 00:23:50.734
That preserves Aisha’s ability to request a trip even when the map is busy.

259
00:23:50.734 --> 00:23:57.270
A circuit breaker temporarily blocks calls to an unhealthy dependency to prevent repeated failures from spreading.

260
00:23:57.270 --> 00:24:03.806
If a routing provider starts timing out, the breaker opens after a small number of failures,

261
00:24:03.806 --> 00:24:09.116
returns a bounded estimate or cached route data, and probes later for recovery.

262
00:24:09.116 --> 00:24:15.652
We also use bounded retries, dead-letter review for events that cannot be processed, and regional isolation.

263
00:24:15.652 --> 00:24:17.694
If a region is unhealthy,

264
00:24:17.694 --> 00:24:25.047
we may finish active trips from replicated state but stop new matching until safety and payment dependencies are

265
00:24:25.047 --> 00:24:34.866
safe. Without these controls, retry storms turn one slow service into a full outage.

266
00:24:34.866 --> 00:24:42.198
A map dot represents a person, so this system must protect people before it optimizes them.

267
00:24:42.198 --> 00:24:49.529
Privacy is the disciplined control of personal information so it is used only for legitimate, limited,

268
00:24:49.529 --> 00:24:57.777
and understandable purposes. Precise location should be encrypted, access should be restricted to carefully defined roles and workflows,

269
00:24:57.777 --> 00:25:02.818
and raw route history should expire under a documented retention policy.

270
00:25:02.818 --> 00:25:09.233
Payment details are tokenized so the marketplace avoids handling more sensitive data than necessary.

271
00:25:09.233 --> 00:25:17.481
Safety tools can detect suspicious GPS patterns or collusion signals, but a model should not silently punish someone.

272
00:25:17.481 --> 00:25:25.729
High-impact actions such as suspension, emergency escalation, cancellation, or refund require auditable policy and, where appropriate, human confirmation.

273
00:25:25.729 --> 00:25:31.686
The consequence is that AI assistants operate with scoped tools and minimized context,

274
00:25:31.686 --> 00:25:34.894
not broad access to every rider history.

275
00:25:34.894 --> 00:25:41.767
Without these boundaries, a technically clever platform can create surveillance, unfair enforcement, and irreversible harm.

276
00:25:41.767 --> 00:25:49.366
Trust is not an add-on feature; it is part of the architecture.

277
00:25:49.366 --> 00:25:53.991
A deployed model is not finished when it first looks accurate.

278
00:25:53.991 --> 00:26:01.560
Model drift is a decline or change in prediction quality because real-world patterns no longer match training data.

279
00:26:01.560 --> 00:26:07.447
A new road closure pattern or seasonal demand can make yesterday’s ETA model misleading.

280
00:26:07.447 --> 00:26:13.334
Shadow testing sends live-like inputs to a new model without letting it control outcomes,

281
00:26:13.334 --> 00:26:17.539
so results can be compared safely against the current path.

282
00:26:17.539 --> 00:26:24.688
A canary release exposes a new version to a small controlled share of traffic before wider rollout.

283
00:26:24.688 --> 00:26:28.893
Cost control is the use of budgets, caching, smaller models,

284
00:26:28.893 --> 00:26:32.677
and measurement to keep an operating path economically sustainable.

285
00:26:32.677 --> 00:26:40.246
Prediction question: when a model looks promising offline, should we turn it on for everyone? Take a moment.

286
00:26:40.246 --> 00:26:45.292
No. First measure ETA error, cancellation, acceptance, fairness slices, latency, overrides, drift,

287
00:26:45.292 --> 00:26:51.179
and cost using trace context across the request. Then expand only if it helps.

288
00:26:51.179 --> 00:27:02.166
If it does not, the deterministic path remains available and the product still works.

289
00:27:02.166 --> 00:27:06.296
You can now reconstruct this system in an interview.

290
00:27:06.296 --> 00:27:12.720
Start with Aisha’s outcome: a quote, a safe match, live tracking, and exactly-once settlement.

291
00:27:12.720 --> 00:27:19.602
Clarify scope and quality targets. Estimate trip commands separately from the much larger location stream.

292
00:27:19.602 --> 00:27:23.732
Separate authoritative trip and payment truth from approximate telemetry.

293
00:27:23.732 --> 00:27:30.615
Use a regional boundary, a protected entry layer, focused owners, durable state, and published changes.

294
00:27:30.615 --> 00:27:37.498
For dispatch, explain spatial lookup, hard eligibility, learned advice, a transparent rules path, bounded optimization,

295
00:27:37.498 --> 00:27:45.757
and a temporary reservation. Then earn confidence by discussing overload, dependency failure, privacy, safety, model evaluation, and cost.

296
00:27:45.757 --> 00:27:51.722
Finally, ask yourself three follow-up questions. How do driver leases prevent double assignment?

297
00:27:51.722 --> 00:27:59.064
What happens when a GPS point is stale? How would scheduled rides change capacity and dispatch?

298
00:27:59.064 --> 00:28:03.194
The enduring lesson is not a list of technologies.

299
00:28:03.194 --> 00:28:09.618
It is a habit: make the product reliable first, make the AI helpful second,

300
00:28:09.618 --> 00:28:15.124
and always explain what happens when a component is missing or wrong.

301
00:28:15.124 --> 00:28:20.171
Practice this story aloud once, then redraw the flow from memory.

302
00:28:20.171 --> 00:28:25.677
That is how an architecture becomes an explanation you can confidently share.

303
00:28:25.677 --> 00:28:35.366
That is a design others can understand, critique, and improve.
